+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Kernel Computation:** Computing the full kernel $\mathbf{K} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}$ is quadratic in memory and infeasible for millions of points [S1]. The sampling strategy is required [S1].
+*   **SAM Ambiguity:** SAM may return subparts, parts, or entire objects for a single point. High-variance normal segments are ignored as invalid [S1].
+*   **Multi-view Association:** SAM masks lack semantic information, making 2D-3D lifting difficult due to unknown mask associations and variable numbers of segments [S1]. The linear regression approach handles this by solving for weights per view [S1].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Plane Descriptor Learning via 2D SAM Lifting
-Learns 3D plane descriptors $\mathbf{z}$ by lifting 2D segmentation masks from SAM to 3D using linear regression to map rendered 3D descriptors to 2D segment labels. It resolves the lack of multi-view proposal association and variable numbers of masks by solving a linear regression problem for each view [S1].
+This subtopic details the mechanism for learning 3D plane descriptors $\mathbf{z}$ by lifting 2D segmentation masks from SAM. It involves rendering 3D descriptors to 2D and solving a linear regression problem to map them to 2D segment labels. It sets itself apart by avoiding explicit 3D plane labels, using 2D priors instead. Choose this when you need to incorporate semantic segmentation priors into 3D Gaussian optimization without 3D ground truth. Link: [Plane Descriptor Learning via 2D SAM Lifting](15766-appearance-and-physical-modeling.md) (Note: Placeholder link as specific article doesn't exist, linking to parent context).
 
 ### Local Planar Alignment of 3DGS
-Enforces Gaussian centers to lie on object surfaces by projecting them onto local tangent planes, computed from K Nearest Neighbors (KNN) covariance matrices. It also applies Laplacian smoothing on normal and descriptor features [S1].
+This subtopic describes enforcing Gaussian centers to lie on object surfaces by projecting them onto local tangent planes computed from KNN covariance matrices. It sets itself apart by providing a geometric constraint that aligns Gaussians with surfaces, improving mesh quality [S1]. Choose this when you need to extract cleaner meshes from 3DGS [S1]. Link: [Local Planar Alignment](15766-appearance-and-physical-modeling.md).
 
 ### Gaussian Mixture Tree
-A probabilistic approach for planar construction of the scene geometry in a bottom-up modeling. It models the scene as a tree structure where leaf nodes are Gaussians and parent nodes are formed by merging similar Gaussians, allowing the identification of distinct 3D plane instances [S1].
+This subtopic describes the probabilistic bottom-up modeling of scene geometry. It sets itself apart by hierarchically grouping Gaussians to identify distinct 3D plane instances. Choose this when you need to parse the scene into explicit planar components. Link: [Gaussian Mixture Tree](15766-appearance-and-physical-modeling.md).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The mean-shift update is optimized to take approximately one second on the GPU for millions of points using subset sampling [S1]. Holistic separability overhead is minor, occurring every 100 iterations [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Computing the full kernel $\mathbf{K} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N}$ is quadratic in memory and infeasible for millions of points; thus, subset sampling is required [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The mean-shift update is optimized to take approximately 1 second on GPU despite $N$ being in the millions, due to the sampling strategy [S1]. The linear regression is recomputed per view but is fast [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Avoids $O(N^2)$ memory for the kernel matrix by using random sampling [S1].
 *   **Performance:** On ScanNet, PGS achieves an Accuracy of 0.137 and Completeness of 0.118, outperforming PlanarRecon (0.154 Accuracy, 0.187 Completeness) [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Unsupervised, does not require 3D labels or depth supervision, state-of-the-art planar reconstruction, generalizable across datasets [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** High-quality rendering is not the primary objective (uses SH degree 2), relies on SAM for initialization which may propagate errors if masks are poor [S1].
-*   **Hardware:** Tested on a single NVIDIA GeForce RTX 2080 Ti [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Unsupervised, no depth/3D labels required, fast optimization compared to other optimization-based planar methods, robust across datasets [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on SAM quality, Spherical Harmonics degree 2 limits high-frequency color detail, densification threshold is higher to limit Gaussian count [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When 3D planar reconstruction is needed from RGB images without depth supervision.
+*   **When not to use:** When high-fidelity rendering is the primary goal (SH degree 2 is used) or when 3D ground truth is available and supervised methods are preferred.
 
 ## Background
-Planar Gaussian Splatting (PGS) was proposed by Farhad G. Zanjani, Hong Cai, Hanno Ackermann, Leila Mirvakhabova, and Fatih Porikli from Qualcomm AI Research [S1]. It was presented at WACV 2025 (arXiv:2412.01931v1, Dec 2024) [S1]. The method builds on 3D Gaussian Splatting [16] and leverages the Segment Anything Model (SAM) [17] for 2D segmentation priors [S1].
+Planar Gaussian Splatting (PGS) was introduced by Farhad G. Zanjani, Hong Cai, Hanno Ackermann, Leila Mirvakhabova, and Fatih Porikli from Qualcomm AI Research [S1]. The paper was published on arXiv on December 2, 2024, and later in WACV 2025 [S1]. It addresses the challenge of identifying 3D planar surfaces in images, which is difficult for standard 3DGS due to its floating ellipsoid nature [S1]. PGS builds on the 3D Gaussian Splatting framework [16] and leverages foundation models like SAM [17] to provide 2D priors [S1].
 
